OER MCP — content attribution & licensing
==========================================

The oer-mcp CODE is MIT-licensed (see LICENSE). The curriculum CONTENT it
indexes belongs to its original publishers under their own licenses or terms.
Content is partitioned into FOUR databases by license so that deployments can
include only the tiers whose terms they accept (decision D11):

  oer_core.db    CC BY / public domain content.        Default install.
  oer_ncsa.db    CC BY-NC-SA content.                  Optional (`--with-khan`).
  oer_state.db   State copyright, educational-use.     Optional (`--with-state`).
  oer_ap.db      © College Board, educational-use.     Optional (`--with-ap`).

Every content chunk carries a non-nullable attribution string that is surfaced
in every tool response, so downstream tools propagate attribution
automatically (decision D4). Per-book license is stored on every chunk.

This NOTICE is informational, not legal advice.


oer_core.db — CC BY / public domain
-----------------------------------

OpenStax (https://openstax.org) — Rice University
  Textbook content ingested from the openstax/osbooks-* CNXML repositories.
  License is per-book, read from each book's collection.xml at ingestion and
  stored on every chunk. Titles carrying CC BY 4.0 land here; CC BY-NC-SA
  titles are partitioned into oer_ncsa.db (below).
    CC BY 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Illustrative Mathematics (https://illustrativemathematics.org)
  K-12 lessons with publisher "Addressing" CCSS tags. CC BY 4.0.
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Smarter Balanced (https://smarterbalanced.org)
  Released sample items (grades 3-8 + HS) from the Smarter Balanced sample-item
  API, with publisher CCSS + DOK metadata. CC BY 4.0.
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

NAEP — National Assessment of Educational Progress (https://nationsreportcard.gov)
  Released items (grades 4/8/12, 1990-2024) from the NAEP Questions Tool.
  Produced by the U.S. Department of Education / NCES; released items are works
  of the U.S. federal government and treated as public domain.

Style-generated SAT / ACT items
  Not verbatim exam content. Model-authored practice items generated from a
  style reference, each carrying its own answer key. These are original works
  covered by this repository's MIT license (item_generation='style_generated').


